[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 457,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 370,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64598,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 370,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Adenosquamous and mucoepidermoid carcinomas are malignant epithelial tumours exhibiting both squamous and glandular differentiation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 458,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 371,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64599,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 371,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8560/3 Adenosquamous carcinoma</p>\r\n<p>8430/3 Mucoepidermoid carcinoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 459,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 372,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64600,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 372,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2C77.2 Adenosquamous carcinoma of cervix uteri</p>\r\n<p>2C77.Y &amp; XH1J36 Other specified malignant neoplasms of cervix uteri &amp; Mucoepidermoid carcinoma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 460,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 373,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64601,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 373,
        "headingText": "<p><em>Not recommended:</em> glassy cell carcinoma</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-24T14:50:20"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 461,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 374,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64602,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 374,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 462,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 375,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64603,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most are located at the squamocolumnar junction.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64604,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The mean patient age at presentation is approximately 46&nbsp;years {30258209}. Most patients present with abnormal vaginal bleeding.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64605,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "<p>These tumours are uncommon but not rare, accounting for about 5&ndash;6% of all cervical cancers {30258209; 3133103; 7211986; 7095573; 4370252}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-03T17:04:53"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64606,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These tumours are associated with high-risk HPV, most commonly types 16 and 18 {30258209}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64607,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In situ hybridization or PCR can be used to detect HPV. Compared with squamous carcinoma, adenosquamous carcinoma exhibits lower ARID1A expression {26303865}, whereas expression of EGFR (HER1) and PDGFRA is common {19563658} in the absence of activating mutations {22274316}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64608,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumour appears as an ulcerated, nodular, or polypoid firm mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64609,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Histologically, adenosquamous carcinomas should exhibit unequivocal areas of glandular and squamous differentiation. The glandular component is typically of usual HPV-associated type. The squamous cells may exhibit abundant clear, glycogen-rich cytoplasm. The two tumour components are typically intimately admixed and should be recognizable on morphology without the need for special stains; scattered mucin-producing cells in a squamous carcinoma should not result in diagnosis of an adenosquamous carcinoma. Areas of necrosis, inflammatory infiltrate, and lymphovascular invasion are frequently present. &ldquo;Glassy cell carcinoma&rdquo; is a term that has been used for poorly differentiated adenosquamous carcinomas, but this terminology is not recommended. Adenosquamous carcinoma should be distinguished from invasive stratified mucin-producing carcinoma and cervical mucoepidermoid carcinoma&nbsp;&ndash; the latter being a very rare entity that exhibits three cell types (epidermoid, mucin-producing, and intermediate) and shows <em>MAML2</em> gene rearrangements {19092631}.</p>\r\n<p>Both of the tumour components in adenosquamous carcinoma usually exhibit diffuse immunoreactivity for p16. The glandular component is typically positive for CK7, CEA, and PAX8, and the squamous component is usually positive for p63 and p40.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-02T17:26:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64610,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The finding of both malignant glandular cells and malignant squamous cells can raise the possibility of adenosquamous carcinoma. However, classification is best deferred to a histological specimen.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64611,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64612,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> malignant glandular and squamous elements.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64613,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The staging is the same as for other cervical neoplasms.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 64614,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 309,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Older literature suggested an aggressive clinical course and that adenosquamous carcinoma had a worse prognosis, stage for stage, than squamous carcinoma and adenocarcinoma {8946863; 12712471; 14749637; 27646608; 28028992; 24407572; 25312397}. However, recent studies have shown a clinical outcome similar to that of cervical adenocarcinoma {30258209}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-04-28T15:12:49"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]